  "result": "Fixed. The quote command now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"5\"` into `\"05\"`, so `\"12.5\"` was read as 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250. That's why the command printed 26.09: 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax. Pricing and tax were fine. I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Prices with two decimals like `\"12.34\"` were never affected, which is probably why the tests didn't catch it.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 pass. None of them covers a price with one decimal, so a one-line test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would stop this coming back. I can add it if you want.\n\nI also noticed two other problems in `to_cents` that I didn't fix:\n- Negative amounts come out wrong: `\"-1.50\"` gives -50 instead of -150.\n- An input with no whole part, like `\".5\"`, crashes with a `ValueError`.",
